ry  music and wine having spent a career  practicing law in california mr mcginty  has now retired to scottsdale arizona  where he continues to write on history  and the law while pursuing many other  interests ladies and gentlemen it is now  my great pleasure to introduce and would  you please make a warm welcome for mr  brian mcginty  [Applause]  thank you ken sullivan thank you  judge sanders a wonderful introduction  judge sanders felt that i added some  depth to the tour of the courthouse that  he conducted just about one year ago  i think that he added a great deal of  depth to my understanding of the trial  of john brown and i considered a  fortunate day when i met judge sanders  here and he asked me if i would be  interested in coming back  a year hence  when the sesquicentennial commemoration  of john brown's activities in jefferson  county would take place  after arrangements very kindly  facilitated by  ken sullivan and the west virginia  humanities council i agreed to do so and  looking out  across this historic courtroom tonight  i'm very happy that i accepted that  invitation  i had been prepared to make a few  observations about this courthouse and  this courtroom  which are really really very historic  both  because of john brown's uh trial that  took place here but for other reasons as  well  judge sanders has covered that very well  i'm going to get right to the story of  the trial  and at the outset i want to tell you  the thesis that i'm going to  express tonight which is the thesis of  my book  titled john brown's trial it is that  john brown's trial  was the most important trial  in the history of the united states  that's my conclusion after studying it  as closely as i could and that's the uh  purport of my book i've tried to explain  in my book why i believe that to be true  and i'll try to give you a suggestion of  why i believe that to be true in my  remarks here tonight  john brown's trial was the first hist  trial in the history of the united  states to receive massive attention from  national media  it was the first trial in which a  defendant was executed for treason  against a state  as opposed to treason against the united  states  it was the first trial in which an  accused defendant appealed to a  higher law  to justify violent crimes  it was a trial that involved more  than just a determination of an  individual's guilt or innocence  according to  laws laid down in statute books and in  case reports  it was a trial that pitted two starkly  different moral visions against each  other  one of these  uh visions  defended  the institution of chattel slavery  as traditional  necessary  just and worthy of protection from  outside interference particularly from  the outside interference of northern  abolitionists  like john brown the other con condemned  slavery  as an affront to human rights  a violation of god's law  and an embarrassment to a nation that  claimed to be founded  on the self-evident truth  that all men are created equal  in the course of the trial brown did not  argue that he was  innocent of violating the laws of  virginia  laws that prohibited murder  and inciting slaves to rebellion  and committing treason against the  commonwealth of virginia instead he  argued that the laws of virginia that  supported  and protected slavery laws that  condemned hundreds of thousands of men  women and children  to lives of perpetual  oppression and degradation solely  because of their racial backgrounds  were unjust  the trial gave brown a unique  opportunity to express his views on the  subject and he did so in a manner that  inspired americans all over the country  many even in virginia  with the realization that he was a man  of courage  principle  conviction and humanitarian impulses  however much they disagreed with him  however deeply  they condemned his resort to violence in  harpers ferry his trial revealed that he  was not  a mere  thug or cutthroat as many both north and  south had at