e were excluded  virginia law also provided that each of  the men was entitled to be tried  separately  if the trial of any one of them was  delayed beyond november 10th he would  have to be held in jail until the  circuit court returned to charlestown  the following april  during the intervening six months the  untried prisoners would have to be  guarded  against a possible rescue attempt from  the north  this was a prospect that local officials  considered a real possibility  as did governor wise who ordered  hundreds of militiamen to charlestown to  guard the courthouse  and the jail against possible attack  so parker urged on by the prosecutors  and by governor wise  proceeded to conduct the trials with all  possible haste  in one of his letters to governor wise  andrew hunter wrote that parker was  quote  for observing all the judicial decencies  unquote  so am i hunter told the governor but at  double quick  time  and so the trial got underway even  before brown's  own attorneys  were able to arrive in charlestown when  the boyish george hoyt arrived from  boston brown's appointed attorneys  withdrew from the case  leaving the 21 year old novice to try to  resurrect the shambles that the trial  had become  and by the time that brown's chosen  attorneys from ohio and washington dc  uh hiram griswold and samuel chilton  arrived in the scene the witnesses had  all been examined and there was nothing  left to do but present closing arguments  and so the trial  hurtled forward toward the verdict  the haste with which the trial was  conducted was not judge parker's  particular fault he was a good judge the  heir of a long and distinguished line of  virginia jurists and judge sanders  alluded to his father  judge richard parker senior he was born  in richmond in 1810 he graduated from  the university of virginia and he  practiced law in winchester and  charlestown before serving for 10 years  as military store keeper and paymaster  in harpers ferry he was a  elected 