box to find and view recent and featured  programs  october 16th marked the 150th  anniversary of abolitionist john brown's  attack on the u.s armory and arsenal in  harpers ferry west virginia  brown went to trial following his  capture and was hanged in charlestown  west virginia on december 2nd 1859.  author brian mcginty discusses the john  brown trial from the courthouse where it  took place in charlestown west virginia  good evening everyone and welcome to the  courthouse in jefferson county the  circuit court of jefferson county before  i begin any remarks i'd like to  notice that  the courthouse has just now reached  the final stage of what has been six  months of renovations  kirk davis who is with the county here  capital improvement for jefferson county  has been shepherding this project along  and this is the first meeting of any  nature that we've had here in the newly  refurbished  courtroom so  we're very proud of it and of all the  work that kirk there kirk is standing  there kirk wants you to raise your hand  there's kirk  before i introduce tonight's speaker  i've been asked to give some background  about the courthouse itself  in 1803 two years after jefferson county  was created the first courthouse was  built on this spot on land donated by  george washington's younger brother  charles it was a modest structure and it  housed the county court which was  equivalent to a county commission in a  magistrate court  in 1812 a superior court was created in  virginia and it was for this district it  was housed in winchester  and it wasn't until 1831 oh by the way i  remind myself please turn off your cell  phones in any device any electrical  devices please go ahead and turn them  off if you would please  it was 1812 when superior court was set  up in winchester and it would be 1831  before a moveable or a circuit superior  court was created in the commonwealth of  virginia for this district and its first  session was in may of 1831 presided over  by judge richard parker of winchester  who was the father and namesake of the  judge who would try john brown  in 1836 the first courthouse was pulled  down and a larger one was constructed in  the greek revival style that had been  popularized by thomas jefferson and  which characterizes virginia courthouses  from this period the new courthouse had  a portico topped by a bell tower  pot-bellied stoves heated the building  which had an assembly hall on its second  floor and a stable in the rear  the ground floor was taken up by a huge  courtroom and it was this ground floor  courtroom in which john brown was tried  in october of  1859 now during the civil war  particularly in 1863 the courthouse was  heavily damaged by shelling  and luckily the court records had been  taken  by wagon to lexington for safe keeping  and thus survived the war but by the  wars in the courthouse was a sad empty  and ruthless ruin  a photograph from that time shows that  there were tombstones that were being  sold in the courthouse yard in front and  you can see the rafters the bear rafters  above  in 1866 a new courthouse was built in  shepherdstown which had been made the  county seat  by the federal troops occupying the  county in 1869 county officials tried to  sell the brick and timbers of the old  courthouse in the jail for scrap  but charlestown residents argued that  the building was theirs and not the  counties to sell  the bitter struggle as to which town  would be the county seat was decided by  the west virginia supreme court in july  of 1871 and a disappointed shepherdstown  almost seceded from the county  the ruined courthouse would now be  reconstructed it would be bigger  stronger taller the bell tower was  increased fantastically in size and a  clock was added weight-bearing walls  partitioned the ground floors into  offices the exterior walls windows and  columns were raised higher and to create  a space for a grand new courthouse  courtroom upstairs  the basic layout of the courtroom the  position of the judge the jury box the  well of the  accord enclosed with railing is similar  to that of the john brown courtroom in  which john brown was tried a gigantic  gas chandelier was put in for evening  sessions a balcony was added which was  referred to as the ladies listening  gallery but seems an awful lot like a  jim crow balcony of that same period  they had a different way of explaining  it at that time however  for 40 years from 1873  until 1912 this courtroom would be the  home of the west one of the homes of the  west virginia supreme court of appeals  in its early days our supreme court was  a circuit riding court and during its  three terms each year it set one term in  wheeling which had been the original  state capital one in charleston and one  here in charlestown  in 1922 the s this upstairs courtroom  would see its own treason trials the  mine wars were raging in southwestern  west virginia and union organizer bill  blizzard and over 700 coal miners have  been charged with murder and treason  following their armed attempt to  unionize logan county in what had become  known as the battle of blair mountain  those cases were transferred to  jefferson county and all the trials that  were had including the trial which  acquitted bill blizzard were held in  this very courtroom  unlike some venerated buildings rich in  symbolism and history the jefferson  county courthouse is not a museum but  remains a working courthouse it still  houses the circuit court and many  offices of county government  births deaths taxes land transactions  are all recorded here people are married  and divorced here  pettit juries grand jury still sit in  regular session petty jury still resolve  controversies civil and criminal  affecting a growing population elections  are held in the results tabulated and  announced here this famous building is  at the very heart of our civic life  a lot has happened here and is still  happening here however the lynchpin  story in the entire history of this old  building this old courthouse is the  trial of john brown  here to tell us that story tonight is a  man steeped in the details and  significance of that famous trial  about a year ago i was giving a  courthouse tour when two men asked to  tag along and when i got to the subject  of john brown it became apparent that  one of the tourists knew a great deal  more than the tour guide our small group  delighted in the added depth the tour  suddenly took on this was my first  encounter with brian mcginty  he introduced himself as a retired  lawyer and a historian  he told us of his intention to write the  very book that he is here to speak of  tonight  needless to say i was excited and i am  greatly pleased and honored that he  graciously agreed to come here tonight  on the official date of the book's  release  on this eve of the sesquicentennial of  john brown's raid on harpers ferry  an accomplished writer mr mcginty has  authored seven books of which john  brown's trial is only his most recent  published by harvard university press  in february of 2008 harvard press  published mr mcginty's lincoln and the  court which dealt with lincoln's  suspension of habeas corpus his struggle  with chief justice roger taney and the  supreme court's decisions during the  civil war  in his book the oatman massacre  published by oklahoma university press  in 2006 mr mcginty explored a famous  southwestern indian massacre of a mormon  family and the cat and which took two of  the children captive  among earlier books by mr mcginty are  patareski at paso robles about the great  polish pianist and his love for a small  california town mr mcginty is an amateur  pianist and strong wine the life and  legend of arguston  uh heresty the definitive biography of  the colorful father of california venti  culture published by stanford university  press in 1998. obviously mr mcginty  writes about his own interests passion  and passions among them law history  music and wine having spent a career  practicing law in california mr mcginty  has now retired to scottsdale arizona  where he continues to write on history  and the law while pursuing many other  interests ladies and gentlemen it is now  my great pleasure to introduce and would  you please make a warm welcome for mr  brian mcginty  [Applause]  thank you ken sullivan thank you  judge sanders a wonderful introduction  judge sanders felt that i added some  depth to the tour of the courthouse that  he conducted just about one year ago  i think that he added a great deal of  depth to my understanding of the trial  of john brown and i considered a  fortunate day when i met judge sanders  here and he asked me if i would be  interested in coming back  a year hence  when the sesquicentennial commemoration  of john brown's activities in jefferson  county would take place  after arrangements very kindly  facilitated by  ken sullivan and the west virginia  humanities council i agreed to do so and  looking out  across this historic courtroom tonight  i'm very happy that i accepted that  invitation  i had been prepared to make a few  observations about this courthouse and  this courtroom  which are really really very historic  both  because of john brown's uh trial that  took place here but for other reasons as  well  judge sanders has covered that very well  i'm going to get right to the story of  the trial  and at the outset i want to tell you  the thesis that i'm going to  express tonight which is the thesis of  my book  titled john brown's trial it is that  john brown's trial  was the most important trial  in the history of the united states  that's my conclusion after studying it  as closely as i could and that's the uh  purport of my book i've tried to explain  in my book why i believe that to be true  and i'll try to give you a suggestion of  why i believe that to be true in my  remarks here tonight  john brown's trial was the first hist  trial in the history of the united  states to receive massive attention from  national media  it was the first trial in which a  defendant was executed for treason  against a state  as opposed to treason against the united  states  it was the first trial in which an  accused defendant appealed to a  higher law  to justify violent crimes  it was a trial that involved more  than just a determination of an  individual's guilt or innocence  according to  laws laid down in statute books and in  case reports  it was a trial that pitted two starkly  different moral visions against each  other  one of these  uh visions  defended  the institution of chattel slavery  as traditional  necessary  just and worthy of protection from  outside interference particularly from  the outside interference of northern  abolitionists  like john brown the other con condemned  slavery  as an affront to human rights  a violation of god's law  and an embarrassment to a nation that  claimed to be founded  on the self-evident truth  that all men are created equal  in the course of the trial brown did not  argue that he was  innocent of violating the laws of  virginia  laws that prohibited murder  and inciting slaves to rebellion  and committing treason against the  commonwealth of virginia instead he  argued that the laws of virginia that  supported  and protected slavery laws that  condemned hundreds of thousands of men  women and children  to lives of perpetual  oppression and degradation solely  because of their racial backgrounds  were unjust  the trial gave brown a unique  opportunity to express his views on the  subject and he did so in a manner that  inspired americans all over the country  many even in virginia  with the realization that he was a man  of courage  principle  conviction and humanitarian impulses  however much they disagreed with him  however deeply  they condemned his resort to violence in  harpers ferry his trial revealed that he  was not  a mere  thug or cutthroat as many both north and  south had at