ld not be able to  reprieve him because under the law at  that time wise as the governor had the  power to reprieve or to grant clemency  for murder  for inciting slaves to rebellion for  aiding a betting murder which were the  other trials the other charges in the  case  he did not have the power to grant  clemency for treason without the consent  of the uh  the general assembly which is the state  legislature and uh scotland was at the  opinion that knowing that wise was a  it was a vacillator  and that you didn't know exactly what  his final decision would be he put in  the trees in charge to tie his hands  but that that may or may not hold water  uh  look uh read about it in the book  because wise could have applied to the  legislature for permission to do this  and he did not he did not and i think  that he felt he was running for  president at the time he thought that he  was a moderate southerner who might be  elected as president of the united  states he was a little mistaken in that  regard a fellow from illinois made it  instead  but  he was trying to appeal to moderates on  both sides both in the north and the  south  and so he did vacillate  yes ma'am  what brown was looking for in attorney  well he he left a lot of letters there  are a lot of john brown's letters  and he wanted a uh someone who would  sympathize with him  someone who was a good attorney  but he also he made it quite clear in  one of his letters  do not send an ultra abolitionist  he didn't want an ultra abolitionist  because he knew that that would arouse  antipathy here in charlestown and it  would probably do him more harm than  good to have an attorney like that  yes sir  those were the private attorneys they  were griswold and chilton chilton took  most responsibility for it  there was another attorney in richmond  by the name of green i believe his name  was william greene who was a brother of  thomas claiborne green the mayor who had  represented brown  by court appointment in the early stages  of this trial here but chilton and  griswold presented the arguments in  richmond with the assistance of william  green  and their petition exists it's a very  rare document  i had a chance to read it in richmond  and it was  very very well presented and at the time  the  statutes of virginia provided that the  judges of the supreme court of appeals  were to state the reasons for their  decisions in writing  now they never made  a they never issued an opinion in the  brown case  you know it was a it was a terribly  contentious case very controversial it  was on the minds of people throughout  the united states and yet they denied  the petition without a formal opinion  they did leave a one sentence opinion in  in effect saying that  they denied the petition because the  decision of the circuit court had  obviously been right something to that  effect it was really like a one sentence  conclusion  yes sir  can you tell me if the charges and  conviction for treason against the state  of virginia or for that matter or any  other state was unique  to those involved with harper's ferry or  have there been subsequent  cases and convictions of individuals  treason against the state of virginia or  any other state well i believe there was  a trial here in 1922  when it involved treason against  virginia  so that was another one  west virginia yeah  i don't know of any others against  virginia there were  there were prosecutions for treason  against  states earlier before john brown's trial  but they were very rare there was one in  rhode island  uh which had arisen out of in effect uh  a rebellion there to overthrow the state  government  and another in new york that had  had something to do with the war of 1812  with people who were supplying  british ships in the harbor with  with  necessary material for the war and they  were charged with treason against new  york and as i recall the decision in  that case was  helping uh helping the british defeat  the united states is not treason against  new york it might be treason against the  united states but not against new york  so there were other cases but they were  very rare and to my  to the best of my knowledge that was  and i think it's well documented the  first  uh execution  for treason against the state  yes sir  uh do you think that john brown's  conception or understanding of this  trial changed as it went on yes i do i  really think that i think that there was  a change in brown i think in the  beginning that he thought  that he might actually  um  be held not  be found not guilty  because of the fact that he didn't  really intend to do any harm here i  think it was naive  but i think that he really did believe  that and he did really want some  attorneys who might help him prove that  case  very quickly however he changed his mind  and he decided that it was far better  for him to be executed  for his noble cause  than for him to be found not guilty or  or to escape from jail if he could have  done that  yes ma'am  one of the things that he did  and it doesn't seem like any of the  charges cover that was  well i'm quite sure it was a crime it  was not charged per se  it was  part of the  inciting slaves to rebellion part of the  um  perhaps aiding and abetting murder  because um  there were  four civilians who were killed in  harpers ferry were shot dead during the  fighting that ensued it is uh it is  interesting that there was no  charge of uh  kidnapping there was nothing of that  kind that was  a file against brown  and by the way so the some of the  hostages that he took  and he and he kept them in the  fire engine house in harpers ferry which  you can visit now today  they were in there for  maybe 24 hours some some period of time  had very nice things to say about him  he was uh  treated them with respect  uh  did not abuse them  and  as a matter of fact we were discussing  this this afternoon a couple of them  said well i my my wife is worrying about  