ve would have taken a lot longer  because the federal court  had to  convene it was meeting in southern  virginia at the time and  it wouldn't have rushed to judgment  there wouldn't have been such a rush  to judgment and the state would have  been helpless at that point they could  not demand it but  there was a conference a hurried  conference  in washington between james buchanan and  the  united states attorney  about what will we do about this  about john brown  and governor wise was quite adamant  the state of the commonwealth of  virginia will take custody of john brown  and deal with him according to the laws  of the commonwealth of virginia that's  all there is to it  and brown said well he didn't care  particularly one way or the other so he  let them do  it it was introduced in evidence in the  trial and portions of it were read to  the jury  yes  yes sir  i think everybody's dreaming that he  should have been tried in the federal  court but if he'd been tried in federal  court with the evidence would he still  have been convicted and harmed  yes i think he would have been  oh that that satisfies you  well i think he would have been he was  he broke the law  i mean he broke the law in a violent way  you break the law in a violent way they  don't say  go home and don't do it again they they  in those days they  they strung you up and hanged you i  think that would have been that would  have happened but it would have been a  much different trial in many respects  one one important different would would  be that the aggrieved party in that case  would have been the united states of  america  seeking to  vindicate its laws to enforce its rule  to enforce its order as it was it was  the commonwealth of virginia  vindicating its laws it was a different  focus entirely  and brown would not have been and would  not have perhaps aroused as much  terrible opposition if it hadn't been  that  it was made to seem that he had offended  virginia in fact he offended the entire  united states when he broke the law  in my opinion  yes sir  um andrew uh hunter cited precedent at  one point regarding jurisdiction and in  your book do you look into that as the  murder of the u.s superintendent of the  army  oh yes  you're referring to the earlier uh  trial that had  arisen out of the uh harpers ferry  yeah uh well hunter had a good point  there there had been a murder that took  place in harpers ferry around 1930 the  superintendent was  murdered in cold blood by a disgruntled  employee who had been laid off and he  was very angry he went into the  superintendent's office and shot him to  death he was brought to charlestown and  tried here in charlestown and hanged  and hunter cited that as a president he  said we've always done it this way  crimes arising out of harpers fair the  armory and arsenal harpers ferry have  been tried in charlestown  and so that it's good and uh  um  the the the slight problem with that  argument is if you do things wrong one  time doesn't necessarily mean you can do  it wrong twice  yes  i have a question more about your  writing process in creating a book  since you mentioned that there were no  transcripts of the trial and and i  realized no official trends no official  transcripts and you did uh research  which also involved  all looking at these newspaper articles  and this  sensationalism that was attending the  trial i wonder if you had any trouble or  a great deal of trouble  struggling with what was  what was being honestly reported versus  uh flooredly reported or editorialized  and if you can comment on that well  there is some difficulty in that in in  particular  there were several of the  new york newspapers that were  represented here during the trial  and one of them ran afoul of the local  authorities for of  writing unfavorable reports about  charlestown  can't imagine what there would have been  unfavorable that happened here at the  time but  he thought there was and he wrote some  stories and they took offense at that  and they  told him to get out of town he had 24  hours to leave town he did and he went  uh i think he was the writer for the uh  for the tribune  orth greely's newspaper and i believe  that he went up to  either baltimore and philadelphia and  filed some more stories that were even  more unfavorable about charlestown in  which and some people have accused him  of making up some of the stories now  there were some some were  there were some  good ones in in those stories one was  that the the floor of the courtroom  at the time was covered with  pecan and pecan pecan and peanut shells  that the people were just rabidly you  know chewing on pecans and peanuts and  throwing their shells on the floor and  when you would walk across the floor it  would  sound like you were walking on broken  glass  and  i don't know whether this reporter for  the new york newspaper made that story  up or whether it was really true it  wasn't in the other newspapers  yes sir go ahead  lipstick  shall we go to the signing team  thank you mr mcginnie i think we need to  give him a break he'll be delighted to  sign your book if you care to purchase  one the historical society is selling  the books this evening and of course mr  mcginty will be at harpers ferry at the  conference tomorrow maybe you'll have  a chance to catch him there  for the humanities council we thank you  all for being here and certainly we  thank brian mcginney for an excellent  speech this evening thank you sir  [Applause]  brian mcginty is the author of lincoln  and the court october 16th marked the  150th anniversary of john brown's raid  for more information visit  johnbrownraid.org


